  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We will implement Bayesian optimization to systematically explore the step function space. This approach is particularly effective for expensive black-box optimization functions like ours. While the function evaluation is costly, Bayesian optimization is specifically designed to balance exploration and exploitation efficiently. Our core idea is to use a probabilistic model (Gaussian Process) to approximate the objective function, guiding the search to find better solutions than the initial Thue-Morse representation.\n\n   Key improvements include using adaptive search spaces, dimensionality reduction, and smart evaluation strategies. The method will work at multi-resolutions (64, 128, 256 samples) to ensure good scores across scales, directly targeting the goal of achieving C \u2264 0.3808. This approach avoids getting stuck in local optima common in gradient-based searches.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Representation: h(x) is a step function on [0,2] sampled at n equally spaced points. The constraints are 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 \u2200i and \u222bh dx = 1 (equivalent to sum(h) = n/2 after normalization).\n\n   Objective: Minimize C(h) = max_k \u222b[max(0, min(1, h(x)(1-h(x+k)))] dx \u2248 (2/n) * max_k corr_full[k] where corr_full = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n\n   Optimization strategy:\n   - We represent h at n points by its scaled value (to 0-1) and enforce unit area (n/2 sum)\n   - Use Bayesian optimization to explore designs space (search for coefficients)\n   - Bayesian optimization reformulates the problem as minimizing an expensive, unknown, and potentially non-linear function, using probabilistic modeling to guide the search\n\n   For n=64: start with provided Thue-Morse as initial\n   For n=128,256: initialize from refined solutions or Thue-Morse upsampled and projected\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Initialize with provided 64-point Thue-Morse or generate initial if not available\n\n   For each resolution:\n     Define search space (64-point min to 256-point max)\n     Use Gaussian Process to model objective function\n     Choose acquisition function: expected improvement\n     Compute exact gradient for some points (fallback)\n     Maintain population archive of best candidates\n     Twice per resolution: full Bayesian optimization + local refinement\n\n   Key operations:\n   \u2022 Implement proper Bayesian optimization with covariance matrix handling\n   \u2022 Efficient evaluations using low-rank approximations\n   \u2022 Result comparison with reduction to feasible space\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   Base parameters: \n     dbounds = [(0,1)]*n + [fixed_sum] - but reflect properties of initial solution\n\n   Acquisition: expected improvement with uncertainty weighting\n\n   Initial settings: prioritize aggressive search then converge (vary base_\u03b7=0.8 to 1.2)\n\n   Three optimization phases covering 10^4 evaluations total:\n     Exploration (linear, uniform)\n     Adaptation\n     Local search refinement\n\n5. Numerical robustness and memory use\n   Memory: ~2500 * n floats (n=256: 640k items)\n   Handling: automatic fallback to Thue-Morse if loss of feasibility\n   Sum enforcement: projection after Bayesian update\n   Termination: sufficient improvement rate or time\n\n6. Runtime safeguards and return contract\n   Time management: aggressive multiplicative scaling\n   Core interval management: 100s evaluations per resolution level\n   Fallback: return best known solution at any resolution\n   Requires exact objective evaluation with proper normalization\n   Code must enforce constraints via projection (component-wise)",
